  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nLingering low level moisture has resulted in early light fog and\nlower clouds across the area. While cloud deck is starting to\nscatter/lift, still including near surface smoke using the 12z HRRR\nfor smoke in the forecast through Monday, in which concentrations\nappear to affect especially central Ohio/Indiana, with more patchy\nconcentrations further south. Visibilities don't appear to be as bad\nas with the previous round of smoke getting into the area. Valley fog\novernight may also limit visibility in more fog-prone areas. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2) \nUpper level northwest flow will keep quiet weather aside from\npatchy/areas of wildfire smoke through Monday. As the next shortwave\nand associated frontal boundary approaches the region on Monday\nnight, there are varying solutions with how the situation resolves.\nSome potential for an MCS diving SE on Monday night, but with\nuncertainty of location/timing. The overall blended forecast brings\nit into West Central Ohio and East Central Indiana by 12-14z Tuesday,\nbut the available CAMs show the solution further south and west. This\nlocation could ultimately affect available instability later in the\nday when the prefrontal convection begins, with the potential for up\nto 60kts of bulk shear pushing into the region. Once the cold front\npushes south of the region Tuesday night, a cooler/drier and more\nstable airmass will dominated for the remainder of the week.",
